#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171780 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb1745c5-6485-5a9d-9e8f-19492928e4b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Position Function of an Object Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/id #uuid "dd9a6e6a-e3d1-5684-8a14-7da410461aad" :question/math-academy-id "e-8642" :question/problem "A particle moves along a **clockwise** circular path with center $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $3 \\textrm{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(7,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf {r}(t)= R\\left(\\cos \\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin \\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(7,0),$ we have that $\\mathbf r(0)=\\,7\\mathbf i,$ and we can conclude that the radius of the circle $R=7.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=7$ and $\\omega= -3  \\textrm{ rad/s}$ (negative because the motion is clockwise), we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =7(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3t)\\,j) \\\\  & =7(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171781 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d9865d-2e8e-59fe-9a81-8935df037303" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity and Speed of a Particle Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/id #uuid "cfcca273-3b96-5812-8b0d-029875f0585a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8643" :question/problem "An object moves along a circular path with position $\\mathbf {r}(t)= 5\\cos \\pi t \\, \\mathbf i + 5\\sin \\pi t \\,\\mathbf j$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-5\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,i+5\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, we calculate the magnitude of the velocity vector as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-5\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t)^{2}+(5\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{\\pi }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\pi t+25{\\pi }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\pi t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{\\pi }^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡\\pi t+{cos}^{2}⁡\\pi t)} \\\\  & =5\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the object's speed is $5 \\pi \\textrm{ m/s}.$\n\nNote that the speed is constant, but the velocity is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf v|| = R|\\omega|,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the radius of the circle, and $\\omega$ is the angular velocity.\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=5\\,\\textrm m$ and the angular frequency $\\omega = \\pi \\,\\textrm{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf v|| = 5\\cdot |\\pi| = 5\\pi \\textrm{ m/s}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171782 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17787055-19de-5dca-b1f9-e97a3a7c2aff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration of a Particle Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/id #uuid "5614ccb1-e2be-5944-8229-61d62e5860fc" :question/math-academy-id "e-8644" :question/problem "An object moves along a circular path with position $\\mathbf {r}(t)= 4(\\cos 3t\\,  \\mathbf i + \\sin 3t\\, \\mathbf j)$ meters. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =(v(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-36\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i-36\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the magnitude of the acceleration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||a(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-36\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)^{2}+(-36\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{36}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡3t+{36}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡3t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{36}^{2}} \\\\  & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the acceleration is $36\\, \\textrm{m/s}^2.$\n\nNote that $||\\mathbf a||$ is constant, but $\\mathbf a$ is not.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a = -\\omega^2\\mathbf r \\qquad \\Rightarrow \\qquad ||\\mathbf a|| = \\omega^2||\\mathbf r||.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=4\\,\\textrm m$ and the angular frequency $\\omega = 3\\,\\textrm{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf a|| = 3^2\\cdot |4| = 36\\, \\textrm{m/s}^2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d9865d-2e8e-59fe-9a81-8935df037303" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity and Speed of a Particle Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd0486bc-01ac-57ae-b0b7-5eec452ce235" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171428} {:answer/id #uuid "177138e7-36ea-576a-9be0-ae3fe52ab6cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171429} {:answer/id #uuid "27017d8c-e390-5ee9-913c-ea73beeaf09a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171430} {:answer/id #uuid "398eee85-b5e6-55a7-bc3d-7c18044cf9a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171431} {:answer/id #uuid "4a11faeb-02ce-56c8-b5bd-fe1d3c445fcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd0486bc-01ac-57ae-b0b7-5eec452ce235" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171428} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8583124-a731-5a9c-96e1-bb0dcc3c10ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac5215be-1a4b-5b45-94e1-594b34acf8a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-97541" :question/problem "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, we calculate the magnitude of the velocity vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{2}+(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{sin}^{2}⁡3t+36{cos}^{2}⁡3t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36({sin}^{2}⁡3t+{cos}^{2}⁡3t)} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the object is $6\\text{ m/s}.$\n\nNote that the speed is constant, but the velocity is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n||v||=R|\\omega |,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the radius of the circle, and $\\omega $ is the angular velocity.\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=2\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =3\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||v||=2\\cdot |3|=6\\text{ m/s}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17787055-19de-5dca-b1f9-e97a3a7c2aff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration of a Particle Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87e74c10-6010-5050-b7c0-c210e0b4ea3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6i - 6j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171446} {:answer/id #uuid "8824dff0-6218-523e-89ff-b9077023524b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\sqrt{3}i - 12j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171447} {:answer/id #uuid "9e9b3d2f-dc72-58e6-8e97-ac067586b54e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\sqrt{3}i + \\sqrt{3}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171448} {:answer/id #uuid "3b650509-030d-5225-9e1c-80dea26a5af7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171449} {:answer/id #uuid "42b5db76-f8bd-593a-bb98-2597e94188ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6i - 6\\sqrt{3}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42b5db76-f8bd-593a-bb98-2597e94188ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6i - 6\\sqrt{3}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171450} :answer-field/id #uuid "496df0ec-9beb-508c-969d-793cb6918805" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b6b507b-dfd6-5936-a7e2-413872437421" :question/math-academy-id "q-97568" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $2\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Given that the particle is located at $(3,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the acceleration of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $a$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=a(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(a,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=3$ and $\\omega =2\\text{ rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity and acceleration vectors by differentiating. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j \\\\ a(t) & =(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the acceleration vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,i-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,j \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,i-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{2}\\,i-\\frac{12\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =-6\\,i-6\\sqrt{3}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350251 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb1745c5-6485-5a9d-9e8f-19492928e4b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Position Function of an Object Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cadb88ee-225b-5f6a-9b83-faa4f40265f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350253} {:answer/id #uuid "0638c4a2-ef22-5fcf-9c10-aaf271775497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350254} {:answer/id #uuid "8123a94d-e161-53cf-ad98-e494fac507a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350255} {:answer/id #uuid "279dc438-8380-5c7e-8941-0b06e56e09cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350256} {:answer/id #uuid "6e637d7b-f8b3-5686-acc0-407709a1fba7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0638c4a2-ef22-5fcf-9c10-aaf271775497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350254} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdc807c5-84cd-5557-ad56-9431f3b3b90f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ad5331c-5ea7-58c3-ac36-0eb50db95bf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-97631" :question/problem "A particle moves along a circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane in the counterclockwise direction with a constant angular velocity of $1\\text{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(4,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(4,0),$ we have that $r(0)=4i,$ and we can conclude that the circle's radius is $R=4.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=4$ and $\\omega =1\\text{ rad/s},$ we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb1745c5-6485-5a9d-9e8f-19492928e4b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Position Function of an Object Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3310cee0-0b73-5481-b5a8-33d661cf8481" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350260} {:answer/id #uuid "56a78a97-58eb-5051-9db5-ff2eedf13a17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350261} {:answer/id #uuid "da77c241-2a0f-5895-9a63-22797e533003" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350262} {:answer/id #uuid "2624c744-35b3-5c3a-bb1a-f0db5b535f9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350263} {:answer/id #uuid "ff9c3807-399d-5ff2-ad2b-08a91e302202" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da77c241-2a0f-5895-9a63-22797e533003" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350262} :answer-field/id #uuid "898633f7-3cd3-5dcb-aecc-b484b111cbe5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fdf52021-44f5-530a-8d00-75020fbd64ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-97632" :question/problem "A particle moves along a circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane in the counterclockwise direction with a constant angular velocity of $4\\text{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(8,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(8,0),$ we have that $r(0)=8i,$ and we can conclude that the circle's radius is $R=8.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=8$ and $\\omega =4\\text{ rad/s},$ we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb1745c5-6485-5a9d-9e8f-19492928e4b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Position Function of an Object Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9831bb5b-c63e-52da-8625-408bee1faa3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350267} {:answer/id #uuid "c13535d1-8134-5e24-853e-b6788cec800d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350268} {:answer/id #uuid "afc75da7-f7be-5cad-a005-67e0e8019b6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350269} {:answer/id #uuid "8333360c-bdf1-56aa-af06-d32945c4f67f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350270} {:answer/id #uuid "4a3b2b71-cb1b-5a37-a61a-625120986dee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8333360c-bdf1-56aa-af06-d32945c4f67f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350270} :answer-field/id #uuid "eddf0a64-bdd5-586d-a36c-d9a20fb46603" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6cdd7c2-fbd8-5ea5-90c9-a0f4e83ab289" :question/math-academy-id "q-97633" :question/problem "A particle moves along a circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane in the counterclockwise direction with a constant angular velocity of $\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(3,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(3,0),$ we have that $r(0)=3i,$ and we can conclude that the circle's radius is $R=3.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=3$ and $\\omega =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ rad/s},$ we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb1745c5-6485-5a9d-9e8f-19492928e4b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Position Function of an Object Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "622ec05b-5272-5e64-bd93-d6c85f9c544f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350274} {:answer/id #uuid "19dd7875-2d2d-5cf6-ae5d-5209c135b875" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350275} {:answer/id #uuid "f34709db-a17e-5071-8e27-5425d4177d52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350276} {:answer/id #uuid "7ee87a77-95be-50f8-8e0d-a3d865bd73ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350277} {:answer/id #uuid "3be3e1be-c558-569b-a44a-439f153c727c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "622ec05b-5272-5e64-bd93-d6c85f9c544f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350274} :answer-field/id #uuid "039c7ae7-cd9c-57db-8b50-50fb8bca0530" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c28d54e1-803b-58a8-b2b2-007b4a9cffb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-97635" :question/problem "A particle moves along a **clockwise** circular path with center $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $3\\text{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(2,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(2,0),$ we have that $r(0)=\\,2i,$ and we can conclude that the radius of the circle $R=2.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=2$ and $\\omega =-3\\text{ rad/s}$ (negative because the motion is clockwise), we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3t)\\,j) \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb1745c5-6485-5a9d-9e8f-19492928e4b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Position Function of an Object Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aac47715-5cb8-5345-bc41-5ca1526dca9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=6(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350281} {:answer/id #uuid "e93bd007-10b4-5b35-baf3-e0db7c54549e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=\\pi (\\operatorname{cos}⁡6t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡6t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350282} {:answer/id #uuid "7bec854f-de12-5953-b47c-d6f5b420639a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=-6(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350283} {:answer/id #uuid "f2be6c5c-760d-5f0b-8284-2f73880b551c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=\\pi (\\operatorname{cos}⁡6t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡6t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350284} {:answer/id #uuid "92397ba2-0b4c-5907-b04e-af00d68f85e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=6(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92397ba2-0b4c-5907-b04e-af00d68f85e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(t)=6(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350285} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb64c059-cc21-5bc0-b7ff-2227f7c64280" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "107bad3d-3b2c-5d63-9de6-a92cec6ccf74" :question/math-academy-id "q-97638" :question/problem "A particle moves along a **clockwise** circular path with center $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $\\pi \\text{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(6,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+a\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(6,0),$ we have that $r(0)=\\,6i,$ and we can conclude that the radius of the circle $R=6.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=6$ and $\\omega =-\\pi \\text{ rad/s}$ (negative because the motion is clockwise), we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =6(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi t)\\,j) \\\\  & =6(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d9865d-2e8e-59fe-9a81-8935df037303" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity and Speed of a Particle Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "538115b1-0f85-564b-b857-ebbf2d0d44f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350288} {:answer/id #uuid "418b4b19-b67e-5e2d-bd1b-a065d9959d39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350289} {:answer/id #uuid "5dc585d8-1f02-5831-a505-a5294826095d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350290} {:answer/id #uuid "b4c4c627-db0f-57ad-bcc8-9d7d629565b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350291} {:answer/id #uuid "52eeaa54-b7ea-56f1-b9e8-d89c723f3713" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52eeaa54-b7ea-56f1-b9e8-d89c723f3713" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350292} :answer-field/id #uuid "7de086bf-c5d8-5c17-8302-5871bfb2fc55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "edacd334-f108-5749-a895-3b5b85eb889e" :question/math-academy-id "q-97639" :question/problem "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, we calculate the magnitude of the velocity vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the object is $4\\text{ m/s}.$\n\nNote that the speed is constant, but the velocity is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n||v||=R|\\omega |,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the radius of the circle, and $\\omega $ is the angular velocity.\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=4\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =1\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||v||=4\\cdot |1|=4\\text{ m/s}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350293 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d9865d-2e8e-59fe-9a81-8935df037303" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity and Speed of a Particle Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df1444a8-a824-59a1-8e9d-49e544441b64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi \\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350295} {:answer/id #uuid "73854352-7f76-5bcb-ade4-69448756ee88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi \\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350296} {:answer/id #uuid "b630d3fb-e3fd-55f9-a1f3-99799896c03f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\pi \\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350297} {:answer/id #uuid "f5c8d1a1-adac-57fe-b079-919db5d86869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350298} {:answer/id #uuid "7a384265-9622-5540-85c5-e910ae7e98e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df1444a8-a824-59a1-8e9d-49e544441b64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi \\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350295} :answer-field/id #uuid "e462ceb1-b01b-5c9a-baa3-aae3291ef579" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f49016e0-1c46-5368-90ce-8ec9a1e4ccdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-97642" :question/problem "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi t\\,j$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-6\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi t\\,i+6\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, we calculate the magnitude of the velocity vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-6\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi t)^{2}+(6\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{\\pi }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡2\\pi t+36{\\pi }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡2\\pi t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{\\pi }^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡2\\pi t+{cos}^{2}⁡2\\pi t)} \\\\  & =6\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the object is $6\\pi \\text{ m/s}.$\n\nNote that the speed is constant, but the velocity is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n||v||=R|\\omega |,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the radius of the circle, and $\\omega $ is the angular velocity.\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=3\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =2\\pi \\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||v||=3\\cdot |2\\pi |=6\\pi \\text{ m/s}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350300 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d9865d-2e8e-59fe-9a81-8935df037303" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity and Speed of a Particle Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9388ac87-4c11-568b-ad76-4d785967e944" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350302} {:answer/id #uuid "5c24196b-047c-5cc4-b2ac-e5ebd18462c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,i-8\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350303} {:answer/id #uuid "ba221c4c-bca3-5bd4-93be-7e9f8eefe93d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350304} {:answer/id #uuid "0b3c314e-f55a-53f9-a470-a9cb42e8c20b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\,i+12\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350305} {:answer/id #uuid "7e228115-1486-5642-a04b-52ef7e41a363" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba221c4c-bca3-5bd4-93be-7e9f8eefe93d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350304} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c38bf45-dd66-55e0-90b1-ea3c3d6ad229" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eab4ecdb-5a78-5f89-80df-0b7c97be20cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-97663" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $8\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Given that the particle is located at $(3,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the velocity of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $R=3$ and $\\omega =8\\text{ rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-24\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t\\,i+24\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the velocity vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =-24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})\\,i+24\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})\\,j \\\\  & =-24\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi \\,i+24\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\,j \\\\  & =-24\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350307 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d9865d-2e8e-59fe-9a81-8935df037303" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity and Speed of a Particle Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4776b89-312d-5184-9040-869cab14cfa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350309} {:answer/id #uuid "7f4d415e-38b8-5d2d-8673-bbaf152e1ea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14\\sqrt{3}\\,i+14j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350310} {:answer/id #uuid "9c183267-1dc9-594d-a988-f75b0c8f085e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7\\sqrt{3}\\,i+7j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350311} {:answer/id #uuid "54564807-687a-5a8f-bc77-fd4bcdea3c13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7\\sqrt{3}\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350312} {:answer/id #uuid "82c8c7c6-e94a-5b0b-96b0-ebe8c7e5fbe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,i-7\\sqrt{3}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c183267-1dc9-594d-a988-f75b0c8f085e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7\\sqrt{3}\\,i+7j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350311} :answer-field/id #uuid "009b9535-b7f3-502d-bf60-cc456a35816e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c763e527-cc94-5b07-acc6-096b0d13fd01" :question/math-academy-id "q-97665" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $2\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Given that the particle is located at $(7,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the velocity of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $R=7$ and $\\omega =2\\text{ rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =7(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(7(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-14\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+14\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the velocity vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =-14\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,i+14\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,j \\\\  & =-14\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,i+14\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{14\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{14}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =-7\\sqrt{3}\\,i+7\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350314 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17787055-19de-5dca-b1f9-e97a3a7c2aff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration of a Particle Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44ab6e5f-6294-57f7-b518-1fbe44525f3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350316} {:answer/id #uuid "2baf3fd0-8d84-5ae4-877b-569a5cfae13a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350317} {:answer/id #uuid "8376623e-19fa-53d4-b951-88aa5cc7f90b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350318} {:answer/id #uuid "abb6419f-a600-56fe-8e52-c7db9c99a2c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350319} {:answer/id #uuid "ea6ade0a-7b2b-54e1-807e-0edb1e4459fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abb6419f-a600-56fe-8e52-c7db9c99a2c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350319} :answer-field/id #uuid "839c81de-7b6e-5b66-b17f-3e29701e1192" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "93547497-1bc4-5a24-a92c-ea680655d623" :question/math-academy-id "q-97668" :question/problem "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j$ meters. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =(v(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the magnitude of the acceleration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||a(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t)^{2}+(-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{16}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡4t+{16}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡4t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{16}^{2}} \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the acceleration is $16\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nNote that $||a||$ is constant, but $a$ is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\na=-{\\omega }^{2}r\\,⇒\\,||a||={\\omega }^{2}||r||.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $a=1\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =4\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||a||={4}^{2}\\cdot 1=16\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350321 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17787055-19de-5dca-b1f9-e97a3a7c2aff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration of a Particle Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c709a099-dfbf-51c1-83a0-1774749f6dcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}\\,i-\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350323} {:answer/id #uuid "98b890d3-54f5-5388-b4b6-069620f16038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\,i-\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350324} {:answer/id #uuid "ffa63a01-f747-5722-8358-079f9f614966" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350325} {:answer/id #uuid "1a5b55af-2e8a-55ae-8df8-63ce6cfc4caf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}i+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350326} {:answer/id #uuid "2c69d569-5bb8-554b-80a3-cf88826a01ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i-\\sqrt{3}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c709a099-dfbf-51c1-83a0-1774749f6dcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}\\,i-\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350323} :answer-field/id #uuid "e477cef2-d4df-5e9d-aa37-503abefe3a08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "35cbae5d-df52-57c7-8623-19ca9f0bd498" :question/math-academy-id "q-97671" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $1\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Given that the particle is located at $(2,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the acceleration of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $a$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=a(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(a,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=2$ and $\\omega =1\\text{ rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity and acceleration vectors by differentiating. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ a(t) & =(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the acceleration vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,i-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,i-\\frac{2}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\,i-\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17787055-19de-5dca-b1f9-e97a3a7c2aff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration of a Particle Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8207b716-5073-567a-8b5a-f7e65fa4ddbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\,i-12\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350330} {:answer/id #uuid "36d532f7-b504-56f5-a0be-2a6f1303b04e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350331} {:answer/id #uuid "4e55b860-d938-5d37-bd6d-2600eb9a1b1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350332} {:answer/id #uuid "ab6ec45d-5880-5f0c-8eb3-bb36a257fd72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36\\,i+36\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350333} {:answer/id #uuid "e9ac457c-6532-56c4-8953-9b6f5a8779e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9ac457c-6532-56c4-8953-9b6f5a8779e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350334} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3b3ce91-a44a-5424-ab02-c446ccbd8457" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e24998a2-9b4d-525d-99b9-c878fc24ca98" :question/math-academy-id "q-97672" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $3\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Given that the particle is located at $(4,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the acceleration of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $a$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=a(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(a,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=4$ and $\\omega =3\\text{ rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity and acceleration vectors by differentiating. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j \\\\ a(t) & =(-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-36\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i-36\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the acceleration vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =-36\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,i-36\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,j \\\\  & =-36\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\,i-36\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\,j \\\\  & =36\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350335 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17787055-19de-5dca-b1f9-e97a3a7c2aff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration of a Particle Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78b47605-0617-58bc-88fc-dca883f78ae6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350337} {:answer/id #uuid "2a5437e7-cec7-5baa-b191-606e7ca9f4f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-40\\,i-40\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350338} {:answer/id #uuid "19c43734-f21f-5fde-b8e0-a30f3285d5c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-80\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350339} {:answer/id #uuid "fddff4e5-c024-57f7-88bb-7e5ecc5dbf8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-80\\,i+80\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350340} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ab8949-85aa-53c3-a291-0b861634e2c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-40\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19c43734-f21f-5fde-b8e0-a30f3285d5c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-80\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350339} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef74b1c2-bb2a-5955-9e5e-5f4cef1db638" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9b0109c-14cb-5899-8302-a8286694f506" :question/math-academy-id "q-97673" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $4\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Given that the particle is located at $(5,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the acceleration of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $a$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=a(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(a,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=5$ and $\\omega =4\\text{ rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity and acceleration vectors by differentiating. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-20\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,i+20\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j \\\\ a(t) & =(-20\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,i+20\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-80\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i-80\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the acceleration vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-80\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,i-80\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,j \\\\  & =-80\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )\\,i-80\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )\\,j \\\\  & =-80\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
